In modern MSTest (v3+), you can inject `TestContext` via the constructor and combine sync/async initialization like this:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class MyTests : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;
    private DbConnection _connection;

    // Constructor injection for TestContext (MSTest v3+)
    public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;
    }

    // Async per-test setup
    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task SetupAsync()
    {
        _connection = await CreateConnectionAsync();
    }

    // Async per-test cleanup
    [TestCleanup]
    public async Task CleanupAsync()
    {
        await _connection.CloseAsync();
    }

    // Or use IAsyncDisposable for cleanup
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (_connection is not null)
            await _connection.DisposeAsync();
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public async Task MyTest()
    {
        _testContext.WriteLine("Running test...");
        // ...
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **Constructor injection** of `TestContext` replaces the old `public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }` property pattern (MSTest v3+)
- **`[TestInitialize]`** can be `async Task` for async per-test setup
- **`[ClassInitialize]`** handles one-time class-level setup (must be `static`)
- **`IAsyncDisposable`** / **`IDisposable`** can be used for cleanup as an alternative to `[TestCleanup]`
